{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773779038,"nonce":"xqA+hHQgqybhZPX8Ovpbjreg2jo=","title":"Allow nightshift revise on working sessions (mid-flight course correction)","message":"Currently `nightshift revise` only works on `waiting:review` / `waiting:human-review` sessions. If you try to revise a `working` session, it errors out:\n\n```\nSession c290d997c2a6 is not awaiting review (status: working)\n```\n\nThis makes it impossible to course-correct a running agent mid-flight (e.g. when it started on stale data, or is going in the wrong direction).\n\n**Desired behavior:** If the session status is `working` or `starting`:\n1. Stop the running container (`docker stop nightshift-\u003csid\u003e`)\n2. Wait for container to actually stop\n3. Write the feedback/message to `resume-prompt.md` in the session dir\n4. Set status to `working` and relaunch with `--resume`\n\nThis lets operators say \"stop, you're on the wrong track, here's what changed\" without having to reject and re-file.\n\n**Implementation in `host/cli.py` `cmd_revise()`:**\n- Expand the status check: if status is `working`/`starting`, call `docker_stop(f\"nightshift-{sid}\")` before writing resume-prompt\n- For review statuses, keep existing behavior (collect review comments from tracker)\n- For working statuses, use the inline message directly as the resume prompt (no tracker comments to collect)\n- Relaunch via `host/launch.py --resume`","files":null}]}